docs/implementation/fixtures/adjustment-native-convolution-diagnosis.json:2:  "source": "Actual PNGs downloaded through root agent CUA from diagnostic HTML; this worker only decoded designated files.",
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:45:Pure pixel fixtures verify the CPU algorithms; DOM tests verify emitted SVG primitives, parameter sharing and export preservation. They do **not** prove native browser/WebKit bitmap equality. SVG engines may introduce intermediate quantization, premultiplication rounding, interpolation/resampling, or implementation-specific convolution behavior. No numeric native tolerance is asserted without an observed native raster comparison. Independent acceptance should rasterize fixed color/alpha cards through the production browser or Tauri/WebKit export path and compare decoded PNG pixels with the CPU reference, recording engine/version and measured tolerance. Source image color management, transformed/antialiased content, document scaling and large-kernel performance need native visual coverage. Parameter histories remain non-destructive; no commit/push/API requests were made by this repair worker.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:55:The main reviewer exported eleven production PNGs through IAB UI. Initial clarity was a real failed acceptance: maxima 35 at clarity35 and 57 at clarity70; no tolerance was increased. Default export white background explains alpha differences, so the offline comparator now supports `--white-background` and has an independent white-composition test. With white composition neutral is exact, scalar-control maxima are 1–2, blur35 max2, blur100 max3, while clarity remains failed. Those are observed IAB results, not Tauri/WebKit results.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:57:The reviewer then operated the standalone diagnostic HTML through UI and downloaded four PNGs. Offline comparison is recorded with file SHA256 hashes and pixel samples in `fixtures/adjustment-native-convolution-diagnosis.json`. **Dense sigma6/order25×25 matches identity in all 16384 RGBA bytes (max0, mean0, nonzero0)**. Equivalent separable25×1→1×25 differs from identity in 11187 bytes, max122/mean19.1187. Order13×13 also works. Actual production SVG properties and normalized weights are correct, ruling out missing attributes/serialization. This establishes that this browser's large dense convolution path does not apply the intended operation; the engine's exact internal cutoff or fallback mechanism is not inferred from this test.
docs/implementation/2026-11-14-adjustments-repair.md:61:A fresh local CPU-only run at clarity70+blur100 measured 19.697ms for128² and46.995ms for256², recorded in `fixtures/adjustment-reference-timings.json`; benchmark artifact test passed exit0. These single-run CPU numbers do not establish native interactive performance. Production browser re-export after this repair remains necessary, and Tauri/WebKit pixel/performance checks remain outstanding. The main reviewer is preparing a new static build for that fresh browser acceptance; old pre-repair PNGs cannot establish success.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:5:Open `adjustment-colorcard-64.draivio` in Draivio. Its only layer (`card`) is an embedded 64×64 PNG at 100% opacity, normal blending, no transform, zero adjustments, preview enabled. Background is transparent, selection hidden, with no groups, masks, text, shapes, or strokes. Project decoding and offline encode/decode roundtrip are checked by the artifact test. Source `adjustment-colorcard-64.png` and unpremultiplied bytes `adjustment-colorcard-64.rgba` are adjacent.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:9:For each case, start from neutral (all-reset), leave preview enabled, set only the specified control, then export **PNG at original 64×64**. Do not scale, flatten onto an opaque background, transform, or combine settings. Native UI export/download must be performed and observed separately. Values and filenames:
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:25:`adjustment-expected/manifest.json` records all exact parameters. Each case has deterministic CPU reference `.rgba` and a convenience `.png`; these PNGs are CPU outputs, not browser downloads. Expected pixels import production `applyLayerAdjustments` so changing that algorithm requires regenerating artifacts. Browser/CPU matching is what the comparison is intended to independently measure.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:36:Compare an actually downloaded PNG (absolute download path can be anywhere):
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:42:Output contains max/mean/count/nonzero absolute channel deltas for all RGBA, RGB where both pixels have positive alpha, RGB where both are opaque, and alpha independently. It checks 64×64 dimensions and PNG CRC. It does not apply an invented tolerance or mark acceptance. Save the emitted JSON as evidence alongside the actual downloaded PNG, case, browser engine/version, app revision, and export settings. No downloaded browser PNG has been compared in this worker.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:50:The decoder uses Python standard library only. It supports noninterlaced 8-bit RGBA, RGB, grayscale, grayscale+alpha, and indexed PNG, all five PNG row filters. It returns decoded unpremultiplied channel bytes without color-profile conversion. Interlaced or non-8-bit output is rejected explicitly. Two decoder self-tests verify all five row filters against known independent bytes and verify source/neutral identity. Artifact validation and decoder self-tests passed; native export pixels and native performance remain pending.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:54:The main reviewer performed production IAB PNG downloads. ExportDialog's default background was white, so those downloads must be compared with CPU pixels composited onto white rather than transparent CPU RGBA:
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:62:The actual reviewer-exported production SVG contains the expected sRGB filter, `order="25 25"`, 625 normalized weights, proper `kernelMatrix`, `preserveAlpha="false"`, `edgeMode="none"`, and arithmetic coefficients `k2=1.56`, `k3=-0.56`; serialization did not lose them. Actual clarity PNGs are nearly identical to source even at fine texture where CPU contrast increases by 35 levels. This suggests large-kernel convolution is being bypassed by the renderer; it is a hypothesis pending the diagnostic below, not a confirmed root cause.
docs/implementation/fixtures/ADJUSTMENT-RASTER-ACCEPTANCE.md:64:`adjustment-convolution-diagnostic.html` displays four embedded 64px SVG images and explicit “导出诊断 NAME” buttons. Buttons locally rasterize their image to white-backed 64px PNG via canvas, then download. Cases compare identical source and coverage treatment with a 25×25 sigma6 convolution, mathematically equivalent separable 25×1 then 1×25 convolution, 13×13 sigma3 convolution, and identity. The page uses embedded assets without network calls. It is a diagnostic artifact, not production acceptance. The main reviewer must operate those buttons via CUA and supply actual downloaded PNG paths for analysis; this worker does not operate a browser.
docs/implementation/fixtures/export-verification/README.md:3:本目录的作品、输入 PNG 和期望值由 `generate_fixtures.py` 直接构造，不调用 Draivio 导出代码。`color-blocks-input.png` 是公开的测试输入，**不是浏览器导出证据**。不要把它复制到下载目录冒充实际导出。
